<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <style name="Theme.MindsetFrames" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar" />

    <!-- ── The app theme the manifest points at ──────────────────────────────
         `AndroidManifest.xml` sets `android:theme="@style/AppTheme"` on
         <application>, but this style was never defined anywhere in the project.

         That is not a runtime fallback — it is a build failure. AAPT does not
         complain at the point of the reference; it fails when the manifest is
         linked, with

             error: resource style/AppTheme
                    (aka com.mindsetframes.habittracker:style/AppTheme) not found.

         which aborts `:app:processDebugResources` and therefore every task that
         depends on it, including `:app:testDebugUnitTest`. That is why the CI
         "Run unit tests" step goes red without a single test ever running, and
         why the step that uploads the test reports then warns that
         `android/app/build/reports/tests/` and `.../test-results/` do not exist:
         the test task never executed, so it never wrote them.

         It inherits `Theme.MindsetFrames` rather than restating a parent, so the
         API-31 splash-screen items in `values-v31/themes.xml` are picked up
         automatically on Android 12+ (resource resolution selects the v31
         `Theme.MindsetFrames` there) while every device below API 31 still gets
         a theme. Defining it only in `values-v31/` would leave API 24–30 — the
         app's minSdk — with no theme at all; see the note in
         `values-v31/styles.xml`. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.MindsetFrames" />

    <!-- ── The alarm ringing screens' theme ──────────────────────────────────
         `AlarmRingingActivity` and `HabitAlarmDialogActivity` both declare
         `android:theme="@style/Theme.MindsetFrames.Alarm"`, and this style was
         likewise never defined — the same missing-resource failure as `AppTheme`
         above, reported twice because two activities reference it.

         It inherits the app theme, which is already a NoActionBar theme, so the
         full-screen alarm screens render without an action bar. The activities
         carry the wake-and-show behaviour themselves (`showWhenLocked` /
         `turnScreenOn`), so nothing about the ring depends on this style; it
         exists so the screens link and are themed consistently with the rest of
         the app. -->
    <style name="Theme.MindsetFrames.Alarm" parent="Theme.MindsetFrames" />
</resources>
